tion in conversations, texts or presentations", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Identifies important and specific information and aspects of attitude and intention in conversations, texts or presentations", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "roller skating rink", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a place where people gather to roller skate ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There was a roller skating rink in the next town over.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words you use to tell childhood stories.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "back roads", "type": "name"}, {"content": "small road that doesn't have much traffic on it", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We ended up getting lost on the back roads.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to sneak out", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to leave a place secretly ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: My friends and I snuck out of class and drove to the next town.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to come across", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to unexpectedly meet someone", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We came across a farmer.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to end up", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to eventually become", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We ended up getting lost.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "on the dot", "type": "name"}, {"content": " exactly ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Dinner was always read at 6:00 on the dot.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "rancher", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a farmer that owns horses or cattle", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Luckily, we met a rancher.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud07", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "innocent", "type": "name"}, {"content": "pure; not corrupted", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Those were the days: young, innocent and free!", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02aud08", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " The Present Perfect Tense vs The Simple Past", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the Present Perfect tense to talk about an action or event that is connected to the present in some way. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use the Simple Past tense to talk about a finished action or event <span class=\"strong\">not</span> connected to the present.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "It is common to mix both the Simple Past and Present Perfect when you tell anecdotes or reminisce about the past.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When you can use the Present Perfect there is a connection between what happened in the past and now. You use time markers like <span class=\"italic\">for</span>, <span class=\"italic\">just</span>, <span class=\"italic\">ever</span> with the Present Perfect.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When you use the past simple, the time is always past. You use time markers with the past like: <span class=\"italic\">then</span>, <span class=\"italic\">ago</span>, <span class=\"italic\">yesterday</span>, <span class=\"italic\">when I was ... </span> with the Simple Past.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I've never been as happy as I was when I was a teenager. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We drove to the next town over.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Phrasal Verbs", "type": "heading"}, {"content": " Phrasal verbs are common in spoken English. There are many phrasal verbs. It is good to think of them like vocabulary. You need to memorize them in order to be able to use them. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "There are some grammar rules you use with phrasal verbs.<br/><br/>1.  A phrasal verb contains a verb and a participle (a preposition or an adverb).", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Some common particles used in phrasal verbs are: <span class=\"italic\">in, out, on, up, into, around, at, off, down, away</span>.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "2. Some phrasal verbs are separable (the object can come between the verb and the particle)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "take off <br/><span class=\"italic\">take off your coat, take your coat off</span> <br/>put in <br/><span class=\"italic\">put in some time, put some time in</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "3. Some phrasal verbs are inseparable (the object can only come after the participle).", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">sneak out<br/> run into<br/> end up<br/>catch up</span>", "type": "text"}]}, {"content": "engenn7le08ob02re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Think of a story from when you were a child.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a detailed description of the event.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Try to include phrasal verbs.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Exchange your story with a partner.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Discuss your stories with each other.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I write about a childhood memory.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I read my partner's story and consider any similarities between mine and theirs.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I discuss the stories with a partner.", "type": "text"}]}]}